#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125421} :activity/id #uuid "95082e0a-322a-5587-9f1a-1280f50a5124" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049678 :topic/id #uuid "0a37136e-6c85-53df-835c-5cf2f76303d3" :topic/math-academy-id 1969} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125419 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125790 :tutorial/content "One useful property of diagonalization is that it allows us to easily determine the power of a matrix.\n\nAs an example, we consider the $2\\times 2$ matrix ![](images/53/53b7a9efd9f2ae1ef4adc7cee5f86f784722777b8c29f268dbd0e822ff1354f9.svg) with the diagonalization\n![](images/e3/e3111a91b80d238cf5255eb9a9884dcd3f80e914039c5e789e32c07b55cf5b06.svg)\n\nWe will use the diagonalization of $A$ to compute $A^{55}.$\n\nLet's start by computing $A^2.$ Using the properties of matrix multiplication, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =PD{P}^{-1}\\cdot PD{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =PD\\cancel{{P}^{-1}\\cdot P}D{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P(DD){P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}{P}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $A^3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot A \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}{P}^{-1}\\cdot PD{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}\\cancel{{P}^{-1}\\cdot P}D{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P({D}^{2}D){P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{3}{P}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix $P$ and its inverse $P^{-1}$ will always cancel each other out in the middle of the multiplication! So by induction, we have that\n\n$$\nA^{\\color{blue}55}=P \\cdot D^{\\color{blue}55} \\cdot P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, we only need to find $D^{\\color{blue}55}.$\n\nSince $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {D}^{3} & ={D}^{2}D=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFollowing this pattern, we get\n\n![](images/05/05e10fdc6944077a48b0214a41fae8d418a1b66c8d87a47d652158c84285c246.svg)\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{55} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{55}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given that $A = PDP^{-1},$ for any non-negative integer ${\\color{blue}k},$ we obtain \n\n$$\nA^{\\color{blue}k} = P \\cdot D^{\\color{blue}k} \\cdot P^{-1}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b7628bf0-02d4-514e-a564-669610253cd2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3576 :tutorial/title "Raising a Diagonalizable Matrix to a Power"} :step/id #uuid "934d1edd-bf1c-5c1f-8924-ede352d7d23b" :step/math-academy-id 20121 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125420}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125420 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124530 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124339 :question/id #uuid "dfb93cc8-6bf0-59d4-8fbc-c94f5c0c1a69" :question/math-academy-id "e-5502"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050446}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394724}] :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"} :step/id #uuid "d76255af-54d9-5dcb-9a9c-7b30b6c4a98a" :step/math-academy-id 20122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125422}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125421 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125791 :tutorial/content "Remember that the diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ and corresponding linearly independent eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ is\n![](images/cd/cde63e04857414720bed0cb247e1e0c4bae76b69823f4aeb494cda13c139ac13.svg)\n\nThis idea also works in reverse. Given a diagonalization $A$ in the form \n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe can immediately deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are the diagonal entries of $D,$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of $P.$ This is because the diagonalization of a matrix is (almost) unique.\n\n**Note:** The diagonalization is \"almost\" unique because the order of the eigenvalue-eigenvector pairs may vary, and the eigenvectors may be replaced with scalar multiples of themselves. However, none of these variations change the actual outcome regarding the eigenvalue-eigenvector pairs that we deduce." :tutorial/id #uuid "d23e5c1b-9483-574b-ab9f-f68b126993cd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3577 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6ed440e4-0b21-56bd-92c6-0ef75f28a68c" :step/math-academy-id 20123 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125492}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125422 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124340 :question/id #uuid "737021ad-5198-564f-bbe2-cc5a06ee5a82" :question/math-academy-id "e-5503"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427864}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"} :step/id #uuid "476486fe-26df-57a3-89ab-80eeb17483b7" :step/math-academy-id 20124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124381 :question/id #uuid "c1ebc3ac-7a26-565f-9798-9de531dc6e47" :question/math-academy-id "e-7133"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049113}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427863}] :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"} :step/id #uuid "1265d446-04a7-5d76-a5b8-e7c56bb1b4c0" :step/math-academy-id 26234 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125419}}] :activity/title "Properties of Diagonalization" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124754}]}
